##:
net/handle -- the release of a socket descriptor, for every socket handle of the net modules.

`<net/tcp>` and `<net/udp>` re-export this module (`public use`), so a program that
imports one of them can call `close_socket` too. A program that holds its sockets as
`TcpStream`, `TcpListener` or `UdpSocket` values never needs to call it; it is for a
descriptor that no handle owns.
:##

use <net/socket>
use <net/error>

##:
Releases a socket descriptor exactly once, and writes -1 over it.

The ONE place the guard and the sock_close() call are spelled. The close() method and the
destructor of every socket handle in <net/tcp> and <net/udp> call it. A slot that already
reads -1 is a success, and nothing is closed.

- Parameter fd: The descriptor slot to release. Reads -1 afterwards.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
public fn close_socket(poke i32 fd) ~ | NetError:
    if (fd < 0):
        return Result.Ok(~)
    let i32 held = fd
    fd := -1
    sock_close(held)??
    return Result.Ok(~)
